# Condition variables

A condition variable lets a thread sleep until some state becomes true, releasing the mutex while it sleeps.

```c
pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
while (!ready)                 // ALWAYS a loop, never an if
    pthread_cond_wait(&cv, &m);
/* use the state */
pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
```

and on the other side:

```c
pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
ready = 1;
pthread_cond_signal(&cv);      // or broadcast if several waiters may proceed
pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
```

## Why the while loop

- spurious wakeups are allowed by POSIX;
- with Mesa semantics the woken thread doesn't run immediately; someone else may change the state first.

## Signal vs broadcast

`signal` wakes at least one waiter; `broadcast` wakes all. Use broadcast when waiters wait for different conditions on the same cv, or you'll get a lost wakeup.

## No memory

Unlike a semaphore, a signal with no waiter is simply lost. The state variable (`ready`) is the memory; the cv is only the waiting mechanism.

Lab 5 used two cvs to make two threads alternate strictly.
